<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>Quiz 9 - MTH 305 - Regan</title>

    <page>
        <p>
            This quiz covers regression models, and retakes on Markov chains,
            and multiple equation ODE models, their fixed point(s), and
            stability.
            Read all <term>DIRECTIONS</term> carefully.
            Good luck!
        </p>

        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    The following components will be related to regression
                    models and understanding their output.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        The image below is a fitted line plot displaying the
                        relationship between semiconductor electron mobility and
                        the natural log of the density for real experimental
                        data.
                        This is polynomial regression and has an <m>R^2</m>
                        value of <m>98.5\%</m>.
                        The second part of the image below is a graph of the
                        residual error between the fit and the real data.
                        Based on these graphs, do you consider this a good model
                        for the data?
                        Explain.
                    </p>

                    <image source="images/09-quiz1.png" width="50%">
                        <shortdescription>
                            Sample polynomial regression.
                        </shortdescription>
                    </image>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        For a multiple linear regression model, we use multiple
                        inputs, or <q>features,</q> for the fitting of the data.
                        Consider the fit equation for Penguin data of
                        <md>
                            \textrm{body\_mass\_g} = c_1*\textrm{bill\_length\_mm} + c_2*\textrm{bill\_depth\_mm} + b.
                        </md>
                        After completing the regression, the model output the
                        following:
                    </p>

                    <image source="images/09-quiz2.png" width="50%">
                        <shortdescription>
                            Sample multiple linear regression.
                        </shortdescription>
                    </image>

                    <p>
                        What do the first two numbers of the output array mean?
                        Using these numbers, what can you conclude about the
                        impact of the bill length and bill depth on body mass?
                        If the <m>R^2</m> is 0.467, what do you think of the
                        model?
                        Explain.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Based on the following linear regression, what do you
                        expect to be the approximate body mass of a penguin with
                        a bill length of 56 mm?
                    </p>

                    <image source="images/09-quiz3.png" width="50%">
                        <shortdescription>
                            Linear regression for bill length vs.
                            body mass of palmer penguin data.
                        </shortdescription>
                    </image>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <p>
            <term>Retake of Markov chains: </term>
        </p>

        <exercise>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Consider the following description related to predicting the
                    weather each day.
                    If today is a clear day, then tomorrow has a 60% chance of
                    being a clear day.
                    If today is a rainy day, then tomorrow has a 70% chance of
                    being a rainy day.
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Create the appropriate probability transition matrix.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Without being told the initial state, would you be able
                        to determine the chance that it is rainy on day 365?
                        Explain the process you would use and why you are able
                        to do this.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </exercise>
    </page>

    <page>
        <p>
            <term>Last Retake of 2D ODE Models: </term>
        </p>

        <exercise workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the fixed point(s) for the following system of
                    differential equations.
                    <md>
                        <mrow>
                    \frac{dx}{dt} \amp = x(2-xy) \\
                    </mrow>
                        <mrow>
                    \frac{dy}{dt} \amp = 3y(y - x) \\
                     </mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>

                <p>
                    Determine the stability of one of the fixed points.
                    Note: just need to state whether it is stable/unstable if
                    you don't know specifics (e.g., spiral sink).
                    Show all work.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Remember, the stability is based on the eigenvalues of the
                    Jacobian matrix
                    <md>
                        \textrm{Jacobian,}\,\, J = \begin{bmatrix}\frac{\partial f}{\partial{x}} &amp; \frac{\partial f}{\partial{y}} \\  \\ \frac{\partial g}{\partial{x}} &amp; \frac{\partial g}{\partial{y}}\end{bmatrix},
                    </md>
                    where the trace, <m>T</m>, and determinant, <m>D</m>, give
                    the eigenvalues of
                    <md>
                        \lambda_{1}, \lambda_{2} = \frac{T \pm \sqrt{T^{2} - 4D}}{2}.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </exercise>
    </page>
</worksheet>